{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 25/11","reason":"The identity gives f(1)=0, f(1/a)=-f(a), and f(p^k)=kp. The five candidate values are respectively 17-10=7, 11-8=3, 7-6=1, 7-2-3=2, and 10-11=-1. Only 25/11 has negative function value.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primary","label":"Evaluate the multiplicative-to-additive function by prime factorization.","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primary":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1","reason":"The identity gives f(0)=-1 and f(x+1)=f(x)+x+2. Forward and backward iteration yield f(n)=(n²+3n-2)/2 for every integer n. Thus f(n)=n means (n-1)(n+2)=0, leaving only n=-2 after excluding 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"additive-normalization","label":"Remove the quadratic term","nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_224"],"id":"solution:integer-step-recurrence:6b7244ad0805","label":"Link consecutive integer inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"integer-step-recurrence":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h200","cnm_h027","cnm_224"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_224"],"id":"solution:additive-normalization:6b7244ad0805","label":"Remove the mixed product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"additive-normalization":["math:functional-equations","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_148","cnm_h027","cnm_224"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2000","reason":"Taking y=0 gives f(x)=x+f(0)=x+2, so f(1998)=2000.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1998_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027"],"id":"m1","label":"Substitute the known input","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h027"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: -1/9","reason":"Set x=9z to get f(3z)=81z²+9z+1. Thus 27z²+3z-2=(3z+1)(9z-2)=0. The roots are -1/3 and 2/9, whose sum is -1/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_194","cnm_029"],"id":"match-input-and-factor","label":"Match the function inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-input-and-factor":["math:functional-equations","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_224","cnm_194","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 25/11, with f(25/11)=-1","reason":"The identity gives f(1)=0, f(u/v)=f(u)-f(v), and f(pᵉ)=ep. The values for choices A through E are 7,3,1,2,-1, respectively, so only 25/11 has a negative value.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-factor-function-values","label":"Evaluate using prime factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-factor-function-values":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 96","reason":"Since 2023=7·17², successive substitutions give f(1)=1, f(7)=-6, f(17)=f(289)=-16, and f(119)=96. Therefore f(2023)=1-[7(-16)+17(96)+119(-16)+289(-6)+2023]=96.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"divisor-substitution","label":"Successive divisor substitutions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"divisor-substitution":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"prime-product-verification","label":"Verify a prime-product formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prime-product-verification":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","math:prime-factorization","cnm_h210"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. I, II, and III only","reason":"Positivity and f(0)²=f(0) give f(0)=1. Then f(a)f(-a)=1, and repeated application gives f(3a)=f(a)³. Statement IV fails for the admissible constant function f(x)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_076"],"id":"substitute-useful-inputs","label":"Use zero, opposite inputs, and repeated inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-useful-inputs":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h027","cnm_076"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (k²)","reason":"Set x=3/y>0. Then x²+1=(9+y²)/y² and √(12/y)=2√x, so the requested expression is ([f(x²+1)]^(√x))²=k².","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_169"],"id":"match-the-function-input","label":"Match the function input","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-the-function-input":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h027","cnm_169"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 8","reason":"If r is a root, then 4−r is also a root. If their sum is T, summing this reflection over the four-root set gives T=16−T, hence T=8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h049","cnm_h027"],"id":"reflect-the-roots","label":"Pair roots across 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reflect-the-roots":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h049","cnm_h027"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1 polynomial, f(x) = x²","reason":"If f has positive degree d, then f(f(x)) and f(x)² have degrees d² and 2d, so d = 2. Writing f(x) = ux² + vx + w and comparing coefficients in f(x²) = f(x)² gives u = 1, v = 0, and w = 0. The resulting f(x) = x² satisfies both identities.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1987_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_031","cnm_143"],"id":"degree-and-coefficients","label":"Compare degrees before expanding","methodDirectNodeIds":{"degree-and-coefficients":["math:functional-equations","cnm_032","cnm_146"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_032","cnm_146","cnm_h027","cnm_031","cnm_143"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: sin²θ","reason":"Writing t = x/(x - 1) gives x = t/(t - 1), so f(t) = (t - 1)/t. Since sec²θ > 1, this applies and yields f(sec²θ) = 1 - cos²θ = sin²θ.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1991_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_156"],"id":"solve-for-the-required-input","label":"Solve for the required input","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-for-the-required-input":["math:functional-equations","cnm_165","cnm_162","cnm_h062","math:reciprocal-trig-identities"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_165","cnm_162","cnm_h062","math:reciprocal-trig-identities","cnm_h027","cnm_156"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_156"],"id":"direct-trigonometric-input","label":"Choose a trigonometric input directly","methodDirectNodeIds":{"direct-trigonometric-input":["math:functional-equations","cnm_162","cnm_h062","math:reciprocal-trig-identities"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_162","cnm_h062","math:reciprocal-trig-identities","cnm_h027","cnm_156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 24","reason":"The identity implies f(x+8)=f(x+4)-f(x) and then f(x+12)=-f(x), so f(x+24)=f(x). The solution f(x)=cos(πx/12) has least positive period 24, proving that no smaller common positive period works.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027"],"id":"shift-recurrence","label":"Force a repeating pattern and prove it is shortest","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-recurrence":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h200","cnm_h027"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (5/2)","reason":"Take x=500 and y=6/5. Then f(600)=f(500)/(6/5)=3·5/6=5/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_194","cnm_p183"],"id":"direct-substitution","label":"Choose inputs giving 600","methodDirectNodeIds":{"direct-substitution":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_194","cnm_p183"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. −2","reason":"Setting b=0 shows that either f is identically zero or f(0)=1. In the latter case, a=b=1/2 gives f(1)=2f(1/2)^2−1≥−1, excluding −2. Values 0, 1, and −1 occur for f≡0, f≡1, and f(x)=cos(πx). Value 2 occurs for f(x)=(r^x+r^(−x))/2 with r=2+√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-inputs-and-attainable-examples","label":"Create a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-inputs-and-attainable-examples":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h071","math:real-exponent-laws"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h071","math:real-exponent-laws"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1","reason":"Choose x with x^2=1/2, which is nonzero. Then g(x)=1/2 and f(1/2)=(1-1/2)/(1/2)=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_196","cnm_074"],"id":"match-inner-input","label":"Make the inner function equal the desired input","methodDirectNodeIds":{"match-inner-input":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h027","cnm_196","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, f(-x)=f(x)","reason":"Taking a=b=0 gives f(0)=0. Then setting a=0 gives f(b)+f(-b)=2f(b), hence f(-b)=f(b) for every real b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_040"],"id":"zero-input-symmetry","label":"Use zero inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"zero-input-symmetry":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h035"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h035","cnm_h027","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 8/(4+x)","reason":"For x>0, substitute x/2 into the given identity: f(x)=2/(2+x/2)=4/(4+x). Thus 2f(x)=8/(4+x).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_194","cnm_156"],"id":"halve-input","label":"Choose the input that produces f(x)","methodDirectNodeIds":{"halve-input":["math:functional-equations","cnm_157"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_157","cnm_194","cnm_156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: {−1, 1}","reason":"Applying the identity at 1/x gives the same left side equal to 1/x, so x=1/x and x=±1. Both are possible simultaneously by taking f(1)=1/2 and f(−1)=−1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_016","cnm_h027","cnm_220"],"id":"reciprocal-substitution","label":"Apply the identity twice","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-substitution":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_016","cnm_h027","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (7)","reason":"Solving 3x-1=5 gives x=2. Therefore f(5)=2²+2+1=7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p145","cnm_029"],"id":"2007_AMC_12B_Q09_m1","label":"Make the function's input equal 5","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2007_AMC_12B_Q09_m1":["math:functional-equations","cnm_196"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_196","cnm_p145","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 109; x=25/84","reason":"Taking b=c=a gives a⋄1=a. Taking c=b then gives a=(a⋄b)b, so a⋄b=a/b. Hence 2016⋄(6⋄x)=336x=100, yielding x=25/84 and p+q=109.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_12A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_p168"],"id":"identity-substitutions","label":"Determine the operation by substitution","methodDirectNodeIds":{"identity-substitutions":["math:functional-equations"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_p168"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, √3-1","reason":"For a nonconstant f, composition gives either b=-1 or b²+2a+1=0. In the latter case |b²+1|=2, so the triangle inequalities give 1≤|b|²≤3. The endpoints occur at (a,b)=(-1,1) and (1,i√3). The b=-1 case also has |b|=1, so the difference is √3-1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["At a=b=-1, the iterated expression is nowhere defined, so the stated identity holds vacuously. This additional allowed pair has |b|=1 and does not change the answer."],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h101","cnm_h097","cnm_156"],"id":"compose-and-bound-the-modulus","label":"Compose the rational functions and bound the modulus","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compose-and-bound-the-modulus":["math:functional-equations","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_162","cnm_h098","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","math:function-composition-iteration","cnm_162","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_h101","cnm_h097","cnm_156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. x⁴+x²−3","reason":"Writing t=x²+1 gives f(t)=(t−1)²+5(t−1)+3=t²+3t−1 for t≥1. These polynomials agree identically, so f(x²−1)=(x²−1)²+3(x²−1)−1=x⁴+x²−3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_032"],"id":"polynomial-identity-extension","label":"Determine the polynomial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polynomial-identity-extension":["math:functional-equations","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_148","cnm_036"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","math:polynomial-remainder-factor","cnm_148","cnm_036","cnm_h027","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, exactly two real numbers","reason":"The identities at x and 1/x give f(x)=2/x-x. Thus f(-x)=-f(x), and equality requires 2/x-x=0. The solutions are x=±√2, both in the domain.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_075"],"id":"reciprocal-substitution","label":"Use the identity at reciprocal inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reciprocal-substitution":["math:functional-equations","cnm_090","cnm_h035","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_090","cnm_h035","cnm_220","cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 109","reason":"Taking b=c=a gives a♦1=a. Taking c=b then gives a=b(a♦b), so a♦b=a/b. Thus 336x=100, giving x=25/84 and p+q=109.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_10A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_p158","cnm_p167"],"id":"determine-operation-by-substitution","label":"Use repeated inputs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"determine-operation-by-substitution":["math:functional-equations","cnm_p145","cnm_p168"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_p145","cnm_p168","cnm_h027","cnm_040","cnm_p158","cnm_p167"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1292","reason":"There are 10 identity graphs with b=c=0 and a=d≠0. All other symmetric graphs require d=-a and a²+bc≠0. Among the 11³ trace-zero triples, a²+bc=0 has 49 solutions: 21 for a=0 and 4,12,4,4,4 for |a|=1,2,3,4,5 respectively. These exclusions also contain all zero-denominator cases. The total is 1331-49+10=1292.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"involutive-rational-map","label":"Require a self-inverse rational map","methodDirectNodeIds":{"involutive-rational-map":["math:functional-equations","cnm_162"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_162"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"math:functional-equations","status":"mapped","total":25}
